Dashboard green during outage|The application is unavailable but the dashboard shows no errors.|up{job="api"}|Check whether telemetry is missing, targets disappeared or the error population excludes timeouts. Compare an outside-in request with scrape health and discovery.|Repair collection or the SLI definition, and add explicit missing-data detection. Do not turn missing series into zero errors without justification.|Simulate both application failure and telemetry loss; verify each produces the intended distinct signal.
Cardinality explosion|Metrics storage memory and ingestion volume spike after a release.|promtool check config prometheus.yml|Inspect newly added labels and series growth. User IDs, request IDs or raw URLs can create unbounded combinations even when metric syntax is valid.|Remove or bound high-cardinality labels at instrumentation or ingestion, preserving useful aggregates. Control rollout and cleanup according to backend behavior.|Compare series count, ingestion pressure and query latency after the change while confirming important dimensions remain available.
Incorrect aggregate p99|A dashboard averages instance p99 values and reports misleading latency.|histogram_quantile(0.99, sum by (le) (rate(http_request_duration_seconds_bucket{job="api"}[5m])))|Compare traffic weights and bucket populations across instances. Percentiles are not generally composable by arithmetic averaging.|Aggregate compatible histogram distributions before estimating a service percentile, or use an explicitly defined threshold SLI.|Test with uneven traffic and latency distributions and compare the query with known expected behavior.
Rate spike after restart|A counter chart shows a huge spike or negative result after a process restart.|resets(http_requests_total{job="api"}[1h])|Check whether raw differences or aggregation were applied before reset-aware rate calculation. Also inspect changing instance labels.|Apply rate per counter series before aggregation and use an appropriate sample window. Correct instrumentation if the metric is not actually a counter.|Test restart fixtures and verify the calculated request rate remains consistent with generated traffic.
Alert never fires|An obvious sustained error does not produce a notification.|promtool check rules alerts.yml|Separate rule loading, expression results, pending duration, routing, silences and inhibition. A valid rule file does not prove the expression matches labels.|Correct the specific broken stage and add rule and routing tests. Avoid removing all inhibition globally without understanding dependencies.|Inject a controlled test condition and verify firing, recipient routing and resolution notifications within expected timing.
Alert flood during shared failure|A database incident generates hundreds of dependent-service notifications.|promtool check config prometheus.yml|Identify common dependency and labels used for grouping and inhibition. Confirm independent incidents would remain distinguishable.|Group related alerts and introduce narrowly matched inhibition for dependency symptoms while keeping the primary actionable alert visible.|Test shared and unrelated failures together, then recovery, to ensure suppression is scoped and released correctly.
Broken distributed trace|Frontend spans exist but downstream work appears as unrelated traces.|curl -I https://example.com|Inspect propagation headers or message metadata at each boundary and instrumentation compatibility. Check asynchronous context handling and sampling decisions.|Propagate supported trace context through clients, servers and queues without using it as authorization. Align resource identity and sampling policy.|Execute a known multi-service request and verify causal span relationships and correlated logs across the full path.
Collector drops data|Telemetry gaps appear during peak traffic while applications stay healthy.|up{job="otel-collector"}|Inspect collector queue, export-failure, refused-data and memory signals using the deployed collector's actual metric names. Check backend throttling and network failures.|Bound queues and memory, tune batching/retries and restore exporter capacity. Reduce low-value volume if necessary rather than allowing unbounded buffering.|Run a controlled peak and confirm bounded memory, acceptable queue age and no unexpected data loss.
Sensitive values in traces|Trace attributes contain access tokens or personal payloads.||Identify the instrumentation field and every downstream storage/export path. Review the trace collector and SDK configuration directly, using restricted access to representative telemetry.|Stop collection of the sensitive field, rotate exposed credentials where relevant and remove retained data under policy. Prefer source-side minimization.|Use a safe sentinel value in a test request and verify it does not reach spans, logs or exports.
Causal conclusion from coincident graph|A team blames CPU because it rose near the incident.|rate(http_requests_total{job="api"}[5m])|Align populations, time windows and deployments; compare affected and unaffected instances. CPU may be a consequence of retries rather than the initiating cause.|Test a bounded hypothesis with predicted outcomes and collect disconfirming evidence. Mitigate user impact while preserving uncertainty in the incident record.|Confirm the proposed cause explains the timeline and cohort differences and that the targeted fix changes the predicted signals.
